{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.936Z","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Why:** each cost a debugging round in the Aug 2026 restore/printing sessions.\n**How to apply:** prefer these idioms proactively in any zsh/bash-on-macOS scripting here.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md\n\n---\nname: zero-edit-youtube-pipeline\ndescription: \"Jack Roberts' 5-phase Claude Code video pipeline — full breakdown lives in the SecondBrain vault\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: a7e959b5-d0eb-427b-b807-3a7774559ff9\n---\n\nVault note: `~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/frameworks/zero-edit-youtube-pipeline-claude-code.md` (source: youtube.com/watch?v=SwKW_D964Tk, Jack Roberts).\n\nThe \"ZeroEdit\" pipeline Eric wants to replicate: Phase 0 deconstruct winning channels' formulas with Claude Code → Phase 1 find outlier topics (1of10, ≥2× outlier score, recent) → Phase 2 deep-research + script beats (human rewrites the words) → Phase 3 record own voice, enhance via Auphonic API → Phase 4 timestamp audio + generate synced images via Higgsfield CLI → Phase 5 Claude assembles the finished video (~$2/45-min video). Key rules: no AI voiceover, no verbatim AI scripts, medium model strength for execution / max for foundational research.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_child-dental-plan.md\n\n---\nname: child-dental-plan\ndescription: \"Eric's 4-year-old has cavities; the open next step is a pediatric dentist and the SDF / n-HAp treatment question\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 9422b1a6-8624-4c86-a4f6-3c4b1a4c9fc8\n  modified: 2026-09-09T15:24:09.736Z\n---\n\nEric's 4-year-old was found to have cavities on 2026-06-25.\n\n**Open next step:** book a *pediatric* dentist, and raise two minimally-invasive\noptions in that visit:\n\n- **SDF** — silver diamine fluoride, a brush-on liquid that arrests a cavity without\n  drilling. Trade-off: it stains the treated spot black.\n- **n-HAp** — nano-hydroxyapatite, a fluoride-free remineralising toothpaste\n  ingredient, used for prevention and early lesions rather than as a fix for an\n  existing cavity.\n\nStill open as of 2026-09-09 — no appointment booked yet. This moved out of Tier B\nworking memory on that date to free cap headroom; it is a slow-moving personal\nto-do, not an active work thread. Deeper personal context: `~/DEV/ContextVault`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_eli5-output-style.md\n\n---\nname: ELI5 output style\ndescription: Eric prefers concise, plain-language output across Claude Code and Codex.\nmetadata:\n  type: user\n---\n\nUse the ELI5 output style: ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English, active voice, one idea per sentence, short words, short paragraphs, and only necessary details. Reports should say what changed, whether it worked, and what Eric should do next. Give no more than two options for decisions and recommend one. Keep paths and commands exact. Codex mirrors this preference through `model_verbosity = \"low\"` and `developer_instructions` in `~/.codex/config.toml`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_sports-teams.md\n\n---\nname: sports-teams\ndescription: \"Sports teams the user follows — use for score, schedule, and game-summary requests\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 03577981-6908-4eec-a664-27fdaa0e34e5\n---\n\nThe user follows the **Indiana Fever** (WNBA, Eastern Conference; ESPN team id `5`, abbr `IND`, home: Gainbridge Fieldhouse, Indianapolis). Default to this team for ambiguous WNBA score/schedule asks.\n\nBuilt the user a printable 2026 Fever schedule (HTML + PDF, full + one-page variants) via the [[pp-espn]] CLI skill at `~/DEV/indiana-fever-schedule/`. The wife is a Fever fan — the schedule was a gift for her.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md\n\n---\nname: working-conditions-bottleneck\ndescription: \"Eric's self-identified bottleneck is working conditions, not effort — he needs a thinking partner / sharing outlet, and lacking one he goes looking for people and loses time\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 583baf57-8dc4-46c7-8842-501ae1fdeb04\n  modified: 2026-08-03T23:18:09.932Z\n---\n\nEric's limiting factor on deep work is environmental, not motivational. He is highly productive in short bursts once work actually starts; what breaks is the path to starting. The specific disruptor he named (2026-07-28) is the pull to share what he's working on — with no standing outlet for it, he goes and finds people, which costs him the session.\n\n**Corrected 2026-07-29.** The pull toward people is not a leak, it's the generative stage. His own observation: alone he wants company; in company the project ideas arrive. Solitude is where he refines, not where he generates. A long unbroken solo block with nothing in the hopper is waiting, not deep work.\n\n**Second brake, same day:** he holds ideas back from fear of overstepping into others' lanes, against an unstated expectation that he act unasked. He is deliberately moving off this position (\"if I don't act, ideas wither\"). Pressfield's Resistance is his frame for it.\n\n**Qualified 2026-08-03.** The permission gate is not entirely self-imposed. He acted unasked on a device another staffer was already championing (ShiftWave chair, second trial unit) and took real organizational blowback for it — careful, low-key handling bought nothing, because the claim was made by initiating, not by presenting. So the caution is warranted on ground someone has visibly attached themselves to, and unwarranted everywhere else. His same-day reaction (\"pander to the people in power, keep your head down\") is a bruise, not necessarily a settled position — don't treat it as one.\n\n**Why:** Both reframe what looks like procrastination. The first is an unmet structural need (input/ideation) improvising in real time; the second is a self-imposed permission gate that reads as professional courtesy. Neither is a discipline failure, and treating them as one produces useless advice.\n\n**How to apply:** Act as the thinking partner in-session — reflect his reasoning back, name what's interesting in it, and give him something to react to rather than only executing tasks silently. When he's ideating, don't rush to implementation; the ideation *is* the need being met, and it's the stage that feeds everything else — help him capture what surfaces. Prefer short, well-scoped blocks with a clear starting line. When he hesitates on whether something is his to do, first ask whether anyone has visibly claimed it — if not, name the permission gate rather than validating the caution; if someone has, the hesitation is a correct read and the useful move is how to approach the owner, not encouragement to proceed. Related: [[company-generates-solitude-refines]], [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]], [[ownership-not-merit-governs-the-reaction]], [[design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline]] in the SecondBrain vault.\n\n\n## Instructions\n\nLook for:\n- Direct contradictions (one file says X, another says not-X)\n- The same fact stated in two files, where one is more current than the other\n- Statements that name a path, tool, schedule or version that another file says has moved,\n  been retired, or been replaced\n- Advice in one file that a later file's experience overturns\n\nFor each issue found, output EXACTLY one line in this format:\nCONTRADICTION: [file1] vs [file2] - description of the conflict\nREDUNDANT: [file1] vs [file2] - what is duplicated and which looks newer\nSTALE: [file] - what it asserts and what supersedes it\n\nIf no issues found, output exactly: NO_ISSUES\n\nDo NOT output anything else - no preamble, no explanation, just the formatted lines.\n\n**Assistant:** CONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs DEV/project_secondbrain-vault-location.md - backup file puts the vault at `~/Desktop/SecondBrain`; the location file says it moved to `~/SecondBrain` on 2026-08-14 and that the old path no longer exists.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - backup file says there is no scheduler/cron and the vault is backed up by hand via `./backup.sh`; the nightly launchd `daily-backup.ps1` commits and pushes SecondBrain through its hardcoded `Sync-Repo` list.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs DEV/project_status-report-sourcing.md - the cadence doc described here carries 9 KRs, which status-report-sourcing says is the superseded v2 set and will produce wrong facts against the v3 doc's 14 KRs.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_okr-staff-meeting-prep.md vs DEV/project_status-report-sourcing.md - the KR deep-dive artifact states Eric's lean on \"all 9 KRs\"; the v3 OKR doc that is the required source has 14.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs global-memory/MEMORY.md - says \"Eric DRI on all 9 KRs; AJ/Blake co-own\"; MEMORY.md records Blake has LEFT and AJ owns FB.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_vendored-skill-install.md vs DEV/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md - vendored-skill-install makes `~/.agents/skills/` the canonical store with `~/.claude/skills/` symlinked into it; the 2026-09-12 architecture decision inverts this, making `~/.claude/skills/` canonical and `~/.agents/`/`~/.codex/` generated views.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/feedback_daily-brief-and-execution-partner.md vs DEV/user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md - one says when he idea-dumps, distill immediately into an ordered actionable list and drive execution; the other says do not rush to implementation because the ideation stage is itself the unmet need.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/user_eli5-output-style.md vs DEV/project_supervisor-log-pipeline.md - the standing ASD-STE/plain-English style strips his contractions and makes the supervisor email stop sounding like him, which the pipeline file says must be corrected.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_command-centre.md vs DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - command-centre says the manifest `class` field routes destinations (institutional also copies to Baylor OneDrive); the 2026-09-09 finding is that `class` is read nowhere and only `destinations` drives behavior.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_hook-two-copies-trap.md vs DEV/project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md - hook-two-copies says always patch both `~/.claude/hooks/` and `~/.codex/hooks/` copies; the timeout-cap file says `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` is now a symlink to the Claude copy so the pair cannot drift.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/project_facecomps-practice-simulator.md vs DEV/project_dormant-threads.md - facecomps declares the planning layer ACTIVE with a next deliverable; dormant-threads (newer) lists FaceComps as LOCKED 2026-06-15, do not modify without Eric saying so.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_cfb-forecast-capture.md vs DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - backup-sync says the Windows `CFB-ForecastCaptureReminder` task touches none of the shared repos and is harmless; forecast-capture records that Windows actually captured the 08-26 and 08-31 weeks into the same shared `weekly_forecast_log.csv`.\nSTALE: ~/project_facecomps-practice-simulator.md - asserts the repo lives at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Football\\FaceComps`; DEV is `~/DEV` on macOS since 2026-08-05 and every project was re-homed in the 2026-09-09 GitHub consolidation.\nSTALE: ~/project_zotero_cli.md - describes the Zotero CLI as a plan approved 2026-05-18 with a `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\plans\\` path and \"no MCP companion in v1\"; DEV/project_printing-press-spec-traps.md shows the CLI was printed in Aug 2026 and a Zotero MCP server plus `pp-zotero-research-library` skill are live.\nSTALE: ~/project_catapult_cli.md - names a Windows `catapult-pp-cli.exe` binary under `~/printing-press/library/catapult` and mislabels the `.env` token file as the historical DB; the file itself notes it is superseded by the `pp-catapult-openfield-connect` skill.\nSTALE: ~/reference_okr-staff-meeting-prep.md - locates all four artifacts under `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\OneDrive - Baylor University\\...`, a Windows path superseded by the macOS migration.\nSTALE: DEV/project_command-centre.md - the `/os` view section says it renders 148 skills; the 2026-09-12 audit cut the global roster to 61.\nSTALE: DEV/project_ath-tech-framework.md - lists \"the separate inventory email to staff\" as an open action; DEV/project_tech-inventory-refresh.md records the reminder was sent 2026-09-08 with a 29 September deadline.\nSTALE: DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - the traps section states \"Tier A memory is NOT in git\"; the 2026-09-09 section in the same file says commit `8a3d55c` un-ignored `projects/` and explicitly invalidates that trap.\nSTALE: DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - the 2026-09-04 completeness signal (9 jobs without SSD, 31 with) is superseded later in the same file by the 2026-09-09 baseline of 76 jobs.\nSTALE: ~/project_practice-load-framework.md - says the next deliverable is the day-by-day color templates and suggests a status check-in before the Aug 5 fall-camp start; that date has passed and DEV/project_dormant-threads.md records the work as parked.\nREDUNDANT: ~/project_practice-load-framework.md vs ~/project_facecomps-practice-simulator.md - the color-day system, rep ramp, mock-game rules and owners are described in both; facecomps (through 2026-06-16) is newer and holds the full decision log.\nREDUNDANT: DEV/project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md vs DEV/project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md - both carry the open \"rotate the Fireflies API key\" action; the fireflies file's entry is newer (moved there 2026-09-09) and names the Keychain as the store.\nREDUNDANT: ~/project_catapult_cli.md vs DEV/project_printing-press-spec-traps.md - the v6 stats-endpoint, date-format and filter traps appear in both; printing-press-spec-traps is newer and adds the date-filter hang."}
