{"parentUuid":"7fbd3f00-86ff-4d36-83f1-3a368806ab4d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"41baa281-2bfc-4ec7-bf83-6560077e96cf","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\\\"sharpened, not soothed\\\").\\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads\\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \\\"pass builder\\\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\\n\\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\\n\\n[2026-09-13T04:30:44.946298+00:00] compiler-stale-lock: reclaimed compile lock held 1550min by pid 47899 (compile.py --auto)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":209},"type":"attachment","uuid":"9f7ecb32-c048-4799-83f1-b6c62fcd14d2","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.595Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"9f7ecb32-c048-4799-83f1-b6c62fcd14d2","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"41baa281-2bfc-4ec7-bf83-6560077e96cf","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSunday, September 13, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan \\u2014 the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error \\u2014 the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` \\u2192 `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3\\u20138.\\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 \\u2192 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\\n\\n### Session (23:30)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` (manual catch-up path for the KeystoneOS daily backup).\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- First run failed; second run succeeded after a fix. Final state: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80` (full count \\u2014 SSD capture included).\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Renamed rather than deleted the stale SSD artifact: `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Reversible; safe to delete later since GitHub has the repo.\\n- Archived a copy of `.errors.log`, then emptied it so the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning stops.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **Submodule conversion breaks rsync backups.** On 9/11 `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule inside `LibraryProject`, so its `.git` changed from a directory to a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every backup failed while CRASHBOX was mounted. Fix = remove/rename the stale `.git` directory on the SSD.\\n- A backup failure of this class only appears when the SSD is plugged in \\u2014 it is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Backup manifest still classifies `LibraryProject` as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`); it now is a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way \\u2014 Eric was asked whether to correct the entry; answer pending.\\n- Optional cleanup: delete `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` on CRASHBOX whenever convenient.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":214},"type":"attachment","uuid":"20517a85-850e-4030-a3bd-270d6cb57547","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.595Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"20517a85-850e-4030-a3bd-270d6cb57547","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"41baa281-2bfc-4ec7-bf83-6560077e96cf","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSunday, September 13, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan \\u2014 the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error \\u2014 the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` \\u2192 `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3\\u20138.\\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 \\u2192 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\\n\\n### Session (23:30)\\n\\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` (manual catch-up path for the KeystoneOS daily backup).\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- First run failed; second run succeeded after a fix. Final state: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80` (full count \\u2014 SSD capture included).\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Renamed rather than deleted the stale SSD artifact: `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Reversible; safe to delete later since GitHub has the repo.\\n- Archived a copy of `.errors.log`, then emptied it so the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning stops.\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **Submodule conversion breaks rsync backups.** On 9/11 `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule inside `LibraryProject`, so its `.git` changed from a directory to a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every backup failed while CRASHBOX was mounted. Fix = remove/rename the stale `.git` directory on the SSD.\\n- A backup failure of this class only appears when the SSD is plugged in \\u2014 it is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Backup manifest still classifies `LibraryProject` as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`); it now is a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way \\u2014 Eric was asked whether to correct the entry; answer pending.\\n- Optional cleanup: delete `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` on CRASHBOX whenever convenient.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"uv run python hooks/session-start.py","durationMs":214},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7123380c-da73-4c47-8610-c7a8763924fc","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.596Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"7123380c-da73-4c47-8610-c7a8763924fc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"41baa281-2bfc-4ec7-bf83-6560077e96cf","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":262},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4b07696b-54d8-4ad7-a0a8-7e9bf32869ae","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.644Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"4b07696b-54d8-4ad7-a0a8-7e9bf32869ae","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"41baa281-2bfc-4ec7-bf83-6560077e96cf","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":273},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b1f907fb-40a6-4cf8-9b15-bca6ebe03377","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.655Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"b1f907fb-40a6-4cf8-9b15-bca6ebe03377","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"41baa281-2bfc-4ec7-bf83-6560077e96cf","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).","stdout":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/scripts/check-config.sh\"","durationMs":328},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2688e050-a6a5-40fd-a35b-94a1f35750ac","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.710Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"2688e050-a6a5-40fd-a35b-94a1f35750ac","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n[2026-09-13T04:30:44.946298+00:00] compiler-stale-lock: reclaimed compile lock held 1550min by pid 47899 (compile.py --auto)\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)","## Today\nSunday, September 13, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n\n### Session (23:30)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` (manual catch-up path for the KeystoneOS daily backup).\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- First run failed; second run succeeded after a fix. Final state: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80` (full count — SSD capture included).\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Renamed rather than deleted the stale SSD artifact: `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Reversible; safe to delete later since GitHub has the repo.\n- Archived a copy of `.errors.log`, then emptied it so the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning stops.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Submodule conversion breaks rsync backups.** On 9/11 `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule inside `LibraryProject`, so its `.git` changed from a directory to a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every backup failed while CRASHBOX was mounted. Fix = remove/rename the stale `.git` directory on the SSD.\n- A backup failure of this class only appears when the SSD is plugged in — it is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Backup manifest still classifies `LibraryProject` as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`); it now is a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way — Eric was asked whether to correct the entry; answer pending.\n- Optional cleanup: delete `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` on CRASHBOX whenever convenient.\n","## Today\nSunday, September 13, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n276 compiled articles (205 concepts, 70 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n\n### Session (23:30)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/backup` (manual catch-up path for the KeystoneOS daily backup).\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- First run failed; second run succeeded after a fix. Final state: `status: OK`, 0 errors, `okCount: 80` (full count — SSD capture included).\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Renamed rather than deleted the stale SSD artifact: `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup/DEV-nonrepo/LibraryProject/ZoteroInjestion/.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` (139 MB). Reversible; safe to delete later since GitHub has the repo.\n- Archived a copy of `.errors.log`, then emptied it so the session-start HOOK FAILURE warning stops.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Submodule conversion breaks rsync backups.** On 9/11 `ZoteroInjestion` became a submodule inside `LibraryProject`, so its `.git` changed from a directory to a file. rsync cannot overwrite an existing destination directory with a file, so every backup failed while CRASHBOX was mounted. Fix = remove/rename the stale `.git` directory on the SSD.\n- A backup failure of this class only appears when the SSD is plugged in — it is invisible on SSD-absent runs.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Backup manifest still classifies `LibraryProject` as non-repo (`DEV-nonrepo/`); it now is a git repo with a GitHub remote. Backups work either way — Eric was asked whether to correct the entry; answer pending.\n- Optional cleanup: delete `.git.stale-dir-2026-09-12` on CRASHBOX whenever convenient.\n","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"cf9124de-31a5-434f-823c-55c9cf3a6509","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:13.711Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
{"parentUuid":"cf9124de-31a5-434f-823c-55c9cf3a6509","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"105a430a-8b78-4b9d-8a0f-8409f89101e3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Review the conversation context below and respond with a concise summary\nof important items that should be preserved in the daily log.\nDo NOT use any tools — just return plain text.\n\nFormat your response as a structured daily log entry with these sections:\n\n**Context:** [One line about what the user was working on]\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- [Important Q&A or discussions]\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- [Any decisions with rationale]\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- [Gotchas, patterns, or insights discovered]\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [Follow-ups or TODOs mentioned]\n\nSkip anything that is:\n- Routine tool calls or file reads\n- Content that's trivial or obvious\n- Trivial back-and-forth or clarification exchanges\n\nOnly include sections that have actual content. If nothing is worth saving,\nrespond with exactly: FLUSH_OK\n\n## Conversation Context\n\n**Why:** each cost a debugging round in the Aug 2026 restore/printing sessions.\n**How to apply:** prefer these idioms proactively in any zsh/bash-on-macOS scripting here.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/reference_zero-edit-youtube-pipeline.md\n\n---\nname: zero-edit-youtube-pipeline\ndescription: \"Jack Roberts' 5-phase Claude Code video pipeline — full breakdown lives in the SecondBrain vault\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: reference\n  originSessionId: a7e959b5-d0eb-427b-b807-3a7774559ff9\n---\n\nVault note: `~/SecondBrain/Knowledge/frameworks/zero-edit-youtube-pipeline-claude-code.md` (source: youtube.com/watch?v=SwKW_D964Tk, Jack Roberts).\n\nThe \"ZeroEdit\" pipeline Eric wants to replicate: Phase 0 deconstruct winning channels' formulas with Claude Code → Phase 1 find outlier topics (1of10, ≥2× outlier score, recent) → Phase 2 deep-research + script beats (human rewrites the words) → Phase 3 record own voice, enhance via Auphonic API → Phase 4 timestamp audio + generate synced images via Higgsfield CLI → Phase 5 Claude assembles the finished video (~$2/45-min video). Key rules: no AI voiceover, no verbatim AI scripts, medium model strength for execution / max for foundational research.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_child-dental-plan.md\n\n---\nname: child-dental-plan\ndescription: \"Eric's 4-year-old has cavities; the open next step is a pediatric dentist and the SDF / n-HAp treatment question\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 9422b1a6-8624-4c86-a4f6-3c4b1a4c9fc8\n  modified: 2026-09-09T15:24:09.736Z\n---\n\nEric's 4-year-old was found to have cavities on 2026-06-25.\n\n**Open next step:** book a *pediatric* dentist, and raise two minimally-invasive\noptions in that visit:\n\n- **SDF** — silver diamine fluoride, a brush-on liquid that arrests a cavity without\n  drilling. Trade-off: it stains the treated spot black.\n- **n-HAp** — nano-hydroxyapatite, a fluoride-free remineralising toothpaste\n  ingredient, used for prevention and early lesions rather than as a fix for an\n  existing cavity.\n\nStill open as of 2026-09-09 — no appointment booked yet. This moved out of Tier B\nworking memory on that date to free cap headroom; it is a slow-moving personal\nto-do, not an active work thread. Deeper personal context: `~/DEV/ContextVault`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_eli5-output-style.md\n\n---\nname: ELI5 output style\ndescription: Eric prefers concise, plain-language output across Claude Code and Codex.\nmetadata:\n  type: user\n---\n\nUse the ELI5 output style: ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English, active voice, one idea per sentence, short words, short paragraphs, and only necessary details. Reports should say what changed, whether it worked, and what Eric should do next. Give no more than two options for decisions and recommend one. Keep paths and commands exact. Codex mirrors this preference through `model_verbosity = \"low\"` and `developer_instructions` in `~/.codex/config.toml`.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_sports-teams.md\n\n---\nname: sports-teams\ndescription: \"Sports teams the user follows — use for score, schedule, and game-summary requests\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 03577981-6908-4eec-a664-27fdaa0e34e5\n---\n\nThe user follows the **Indiana Fever** (WNBA, Eastern Conference; ESPN team id `5`, abbr `IND`, home: Gainbridge Fieldhouse, Indianapolis). Default to this team for ambiguous WNBA score/schedule asks.\n\nBuilt the user a printable 2026 Fever schedule (HTML + PDF, full + one-page variants) via the [[pp-espn]] CLI skill at `~/DEV/indiana-fever-schedule/`. The wife is a Fever fan — the schedule was a gift for her.\n\n\n---\n\n### DEV/user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md\n\n---\nname: working-conditions-bottleneck\ndescription: \"Eric's self-identified bottleneck is working conditions, not effort — he needs a thinking partner / sharing outlet, and lacking one he goes looking for people and loses time\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: user\n  originSessionId: 583baf57-8dc4-46c7-8842-501ae1fdeb04\n  modified: 2026-08-03T23:18:09.932Z\n---\n\nEric's limiting factor on deep work is environmental, not motivational. He is highly productive in short bursts once work actually starts; what breaks is the path to starting. The specific disruptor he named (2026-07-28) is the pull to share what he's working on — with no standing outlet for it, he goes and finds people, which costs him the session.\n\n**Corrected 2026-07-29.** The pull toward people is not a leak, it's the generative stage. His own observation: alone he wants company; in company the project ideas arrive. Solitude is where he refines, not where he generates. A long unbroken solo block with nothing in the hopper is waiting, not deep work.\n\n**Second brake, same day:** he holds ideas back from fear of overstepping into others' lanes, against an unstated expectation that he act unasked. He is deliberately moving off this position (\"if I don't act, ideas wither\"). Pressfield's Resistance is his frame for it.\n\n**Qualified 2026-08-03.** The permission gate is not entirely self-imposed. He acted unasked on a device another staffer was already championing (ShiftWave chair, second trial unit) and took real organizational blowback for it — careful, low-key handling bought nothing, because the claim was made by initiating, not by presenting. So the caution is warranted on ground someone has visibly attached themselves to, and unwarranted everywhere else. His same-day reaction (\"pander to the people in power, keep your head down\") is a bruise, not necessarily a settled position — don't treat it as one.\n\n**Why:** Both reframe what looks like procrastination. The first is an unmet structural need (input/ideation) improvising in real time; the second is a self-imposed permission gate that reads as professional courtesy. Neither is a discipline failure, and treating them as one produces useless advice.\n\n**How to apply:** Act as the thinking partner in-session — reflect his reasoning back, name what's interesting in it, and give him something to react to rather than only executing tasks silently. When he's ideating, don't rush to implementation; the ideation *is* the need being met, and it's the stage that feeds everything else — help him capture what surfaces. Prefer short, well-scoped blocks with a clear starting line. When he hesitates on whether something is his to do, first ask whether anyone has visibly claimed it — if not, name the permission gate rather than validating the caution; if someone has, the hesitation is a correct read and the useful move is how to approach the owner, not encouragement to proceed. Related: [[company-generates-solitude-refines]], [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]], [[ownership-not-merit-governs-the-reaction]], [[design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline]] in the SecondBrain vault.\n\n\n## Instructions\n\nLook for:\n- Direct contradictions (one file says X, another says not-X)\n- The same fact stated in two files, where one is more current than the other\n- Statements that name a path, tool, schedule or version that another file says has moved,\n  been retired, or been replaced\n- Advice in one file that a later file's experience overturns\n\nFor each issue found, output EXACTLY one line in this format:\nCONTRADICTION: [file1] vs [file2] - description of the conflict\nREDUNDANT: [file1] vs [file2] - what is duplicated and which looks newer\nSTALE: [file] - what it asserts and what supersedes it\n\nIf no issues found, output exactly: NO_ISSUES\n\nDo NOT output anything else - no preamble, no explanation, just the formatted lines.\n\n**Assistant:** CONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs DEV/project_secondbrain-vault-location.md - backup file puts the vault at `~/Desktop/SecondBrain`; the location file says it moved to `~/SecondBrain` on 2026-08-14 and that the old path no longer exists.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - backup file says there is no scheduler/cron and the vault is backed up by hand via `./backup.sh`; the nightly launchd `daily-backup.ps1` commits and pushes SecondBrain through its hardcoded `Sync-Repo` list.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs DEV/project_status-report-sourcing.md - the cadence doc described here carries 9 KRs, which status-report-sourcing says is the superseded v2 set and will produce wrong facts against the v3 doc's 14 KRs.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_okr-staff-meeting-prep.md vs DEV/project_status-report-sourcing.md - the KR deep-dive artifact states Eric's lean on \"all 9 KRs\"; the v3 OKR doc that is the required source has 14.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/reference_secondbrain-backup.md vs global-memory/MEMORY.md - says \"Eric DRI on all 9 KRs; AJ/Blake co-own\"; MEMORY.md records Blake has LEFT and AJ owns FB.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_vendored-skill-install.md vs DEV/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md - vendored-skill-install makes `~/.agents/skills/` the canonical store with `~/.claude/skills/` symlinked into it; the 2026-09-12 architecture decision inverts this, making `~/.claude/skills/` canonical and `~/.agents/`/`~/.codex/` generated views.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/feedback_daily-brief-and-execution-partner.md vs DEV/user_working-conditions-bottleneck.md - one says when he idea-dumps, distill immediately into an ordered actionable list and drive execution; the other says do not rush to implementation because the ideation stage is itself the unmet need.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/user_eli5-output-style.md vs DEV/project_supervisor-log-pipeline.md - the standing ASD-STE/plain-English style strips his contractions and makes the supervisor email stop sounding like him, which the pipeline file says must be corrected.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_command-centre.md vs DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - command-centre says the manifest `class` field routes destinations (institutional also copies to Baylor OneDrive); the 2026-09-09 finding is that `class` is read nowhere and only `destinations` drives behavior.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_hook-two-copies-trap.md vs DEV/project_memsearch-hook-timeout-cap.md - hook-two-copies says always patch both `~/.claude/hooks/` and `~/.codex/hooks/` copies; the timeout-cap file says `~/.codex/hooks/memsearch-sync.js` is now a symlink to the Claude copy so the pair cannot drift.\nCONTRADICTION: ~/project_facecomps-practice-simulator.md vs DEV/project_dormant-threads.md - facecomps declares the planning layer ACTIVE with a next deliverable; dormant-threads (newer) lists FaceComps as LOCKED 2026-06-15, do not modify without Eric saying so.\nCONTRADICTION: DEV/project_cfb-forecast-capture.md vs DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - backup-sync says the Windows `CFB-ForecastCaptureReminder` task touches none of the shared repos and is harmless; forecast-capture records that Windows actually captured the 08-26 and 08-31 weeks into the same shared `weekly_forecast_log.csv`.\nSTALE: ~/project_facecomps-practice-simulator.md - asserts the repo lives at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Football\\FaceComps`; DEV is `~/DEV` on macOS since 2026-08-05 and every project was re-homed in the 2026-09-09 GitHub consolidation.\nSTALE: ~/project_zotero_cli.md - describes the Zotero CLI as a plan approved 2026-05-18 with a `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\plans\\` path and \"no MCP companion in v1\"; DEV/project_printing-press-spec-traps.md shows the CLI was printed in Aug 2026 and a Zotero MCP server plus `pp-zotero-research-library` skill are live.\nSTALE: ~/project_catapult_cli.md - names a Windows `catapult-pp-cli.exe` binary under `~/printing-press/library/catapult` and mislabels the `.env` token file as the historical DB; the file itself notes it is superseded by the `pp-catapult-openfield-connect` skill.\nSTALE: ~/reference_okr-staff-meeting-prep.md - locates all four artifacts under `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\OneDrive - Baylor University\\...`, a Windows path superseded by the macOS migration.\nSTALE: DEV/project_command-centre.md - the `/os` view section says it renders 148 skills; the 2026-09-12 audit cut the global roster to 61.\nSTALE: DEV/project_ath-tech-framework.md - lists \"the separate inventory email to staff\" as an open action; DEV/project_tech-inventory-refresh.md records the reminder was sent 2026-09-08 with a 29 September deadline.\nSTALE: DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - the traps section states \"Tier A memory is NOT in git\"; the 2026-09-09 section in the same file says commit `8a3d55c` un-ignored `projects/` and explicitly invalidates that trap.\nSTALE: DEV/project_backup-sync-system.md - the 2026-09-04 completeness signal (9 jobs without SSD, 31 with) is superseded later in the same file by the 2026-09-09 baseline of 76 jobs.\nSTALE: ~/project_practice-load-framework.md - says the next deliverable is the day-by-day color templates and suggests a status check-in before the Aug 5 fall-camp start; that date has passed and DEV/project_dormant-threads.md records the work as parked.\nREDUNDANT: ~/project_practice-load-framework.md vs ~/project_facecomps-practice-simulator.md - the color-day system, rep ramp, mock-game rules and owners are described in both; facecomps (through 2026-06-16) is newer and holds the full decision log.\nREDUNDANT: DEV/project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md vs DEV/project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md - both carry the open \"rotate the Fireflies API key\" action; the fireflies file's entry is newer (moved there 2026-09-09) and names the Keychain as the store.\nREDUNDANT: ~/project_catapult_cli.md vs DEV/project_printing-press-spec-traps.md - the v6 stats-endpoint, date-format and filter traps appear in both; printing-press-spec-traps is newer and adds the date-filter hang."},"uuid":"60bec161-f7f8-477a-a978-e339967b0f5f","timestamp":"2026-09-13T07:37:16.772Z","permissionMode":"auto","promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"66a8a06e-2257-4266-aeb9-51449a9bd398","version":"2.1.228","gitBranch":"session-start-progressive-disclosure"}
